            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Protagoras and Education</strong><br>\nProtagoras was one of the first to propose a structured curriculum for education. He believed in the importance of teaching virtue and argued that it could be taught, a view not shared by all of his contemporaries. Through his educational practices, he sought to improve the moral and intellectual caliber of his students, preparing them for life as active citizens of the polis.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Impact on Rhetoric</strong><br>\nThe influence of Protagoras extended to the realm of rhetoric, where he is considered one of its early and most important contributors. He composed treatises on argumentative techniques and was among the first to teach structured public speaking, thereby laying the foundations for classical rhetoric.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Criticism and Controversy</strong><br>\nProtagoras's relativistic views drew criticism from other philosophers, particularly Plato, who portrayed him as a skeptic who doubted the possibility of true knowledge. Despite such criticisms, Protagoras's ideas were crucial in challenging traditional Greek views on morality and knowledge, sparking philosophical debates that would continue for centuries.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
