          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>His Work and Writings</strong><br>\nAlthough none of his works have survived in complete form, Protagoras is known to have written several books, which we know about through references by other authors. His most famous work, \"Truth,\" included his agnostic stance on the gods, which led to accusations of impiety and a trial that may have resulted in his exile or death.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Legal and Political Influence</strong><br>\nBeyond philosophy, Protagoras also made contributions to legal and political theory in ancient Greece. He was possibly involved in creating a constitution for a colony in Thurii, demonstrating the practical application of his teachings in governance and law, and reflecting the growing interconnection of philosophy with public life.</p>\n\n<p><strong>His Lasting Legacy</strong><br>\nProtagoras's legacy endures through the ongoing discussion and evaluation of his ideas in the context of philosophy and education. He remains a subject of study for his role in developing the concept of agnosticism, his pioneering work in the field of professional education, and his contribution to the development of democratic discourse.</p>\n"
              },
